{
  "concept": "A pendulum oscillates because the tangential component of gravity, -mg sinθ, always points back toward equilibrium; for small angles this becomes approximately proportional to displacement.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "beige horizontal support bar across the top",
    "fixed pivot point at the underside of the support",
    "dashed vertical equilibrium line descending from the pivot",
    "pendulum string of length L from pivot to bob, tilted by angle θ from the vertical",
    "angle arc at the pivot labeled θ",
    "label L along the string",
    "gray circular bob at the lower end of the string",
    "bob mass label m",
    "dashed circular arc showing the bob's path",
    "arc-length displacement label s between equilibrium position and bob",
    "dark blue tension force arrow F_T along the string from bob toward pivot",
    "red weight arrow w = mg straight downward from the bob",
    "red radial gravity component arrow labeled -mg cosθ along the string direction away from the pivot",
    "red tangential gravity component arrow labeled -mg sinθ tangent to the arc and pointing back toward equilibrium",
    "small angle marker θ between the downward weight arrow and the radial component arrow",
    "local coordinate axes inset with +y along the string toward the pivot",
    "local coordinate axes inset with +x tangent to the arc",
    "equilibrium bob position marker on the vertical line"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "thetaAngle",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Angle θ",
      "range": [
        -45,
        45,
        1
      ],
      "default": 25,
      "teaches": "Changing θ shows that the bob's arc displacement s grows with angle, while the tangential gravity component -mg sinθ changes sign and always points back toward equilibrium"
    },
    {
      "id": "pendulumMotion",
      "type": "animation",
      "label": "Animate pendulum motion",
      "loop": true,
      "teaches": "During the swing, velocity reverses at the endpoints while the restoring-force arrow continually points toward the center of the motion"
    },
    {
      "id": "forceModel",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Tangential force model",
      "options": [
        "Exact: -mg sinθ",
        "Small-angle: -mgθ"
      ],
      "default": "Exact: -mg sinθ",
      "teaches": "The exact restoring force is nonlinear in θ, but at small angles sinθ ≈ θ, so the pendulum behaves like a simple harmonic oscillator"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Displaced pendulum",
      "narration": "At a positive angle, gravity points straight down, but only its tangential component pulls the bob back along the arc toward equilibrium. The radial component is balanced with tension and does not restore the swing.",
      "state": {
        "thetaAngle": 25,
        "forceModel": "Exact: -mg sinθ"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Drag through equilibrium",
      "narration": "Move θ through zero. The tangential component shrinks to zero at equilibrium, then reverses direction on the other side so it still points back toward the center.",
      "state": {
        "thetaAngle": 0,
        "forceModel": "Exact: -mg sinθ"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Watch the restoring force during motion",
      "narration": "Start the animation and follow the red tangential-force arrow. It is largest near the endpoints, zero at the bottom, and always directed opposite the displacement.",
      "state": {
        "thetaAngle": 30,
        "forceModel": "Exact: -mg sinθ"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Use the small-angle approximation",
      "narration": "Switch to the small-angle view. Near the vertical, -mg sinθ and -mgθ nearly match, explaining why small pendulum swings are approximately simple harmonic.",
      "state": {
        "thetaAngle": 12,
        "forceModel": "Small-angle: -mgθ"
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The pendulum's restoring force is not the whole weight mg, but the tangential component -mg sinθ, which becomes the linear SHM force -mgθ only for small angles.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Recreate the original layout as a clean 2D physics diagram: beige support at top, pivot near upper left, dashed vertical equilibrium line, tilted pendulum string down to the right, gray bob, and labeled force arrows starting at the bob. Use dark blue for tension F_T and red for all gravity-related arrows. The θ slider should move the bob along a circular arc of fixed radius L, rotate the string, update the pivot angle label, update the dashed arc length s, and resize/reorient the -mg sinθ and -mg cosθ arrows. In animation mode, show the bob oscillating along the same arc and optionally add a velocity arrow tangent to the path so learners can see velocity reverse at endpoints while restoring force points inward. In small-angle mode, relabel the tangential force as approximately -mgθ and visually highlight a small-angle range near the vertical, such as |θ| less than about 15 degrees."
}

